#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-artifact-style — PreToolUse hook on the `Artifact` tool: the house style for every
shareable online artifact arrives before the page is written, and a page that would be
unreadable in one of the two themes is refused before it is published.

WHY A HOOK AND NOT A RULE. User request, 2026-10-02, verbatim: *"I want this skill to
automatically adjust artifacts, online artifacts, especially online artifacts that are
shared. Shareable. So when a user asks to create an online artifact or workforce decides to
generate one, this skill needs to be applied so that it gets formatted correctly."* The
operative words are *without being prompted*: *"whatever works well with workforce to make
this happen automatically without being prompted."* A skill that has to be invoked is a
skill the turn skips, and "workforce decides to generate one" happens inside a spawned
employee that never saw the user's message. `PreToolUse` fires in subagents too, so the
rule reaches the main loop and every employee by the same registration.

WHERE THE STYLE CAME FROM. The session that built `efw-orientation-brief.html` published the
same content twice — once through the Claude Docs connector, once as one HTML page. The user
on the first: *"okay, the formatting of that shared artifact is a bit hard on the eyes. Can
we clean it up a bit please? Make each of the headings pop out better and just overall
appearance layed out a bit more professionally?"* On the second: *"The new layout is
wonderful."* The skill `shared-artifact` carries that layout as a reusable recipe and a
neutral template; this hook is the thing that makes it arrive.

WHAT IT DOES, BY CALL. The `Artifact` tool's actions were read out of 66 real calls in this
machine's own transcripts rather than from memory: `quickstart` (`intent`), `publish`
(`type_url` + `title`, the connector-typed create), `read`, `list`, `comments`, and the
implicit publish — no `action` at all, just `file_path` — which is 59 of the 66.

  quickstart         the mandated first call for any new artifact. Emits the style
                     instruction and the routing rule, so the recipe is in hand BEFORE a
                     line of the page exists.
  publish/type_url   a Claude Docs create. Not HTML and not checkable; the Docs viewer owns
                     its own heading styles, which is exactly why the first attempt could
                     not make headings pop. Emits the routing rule, never denies.
  implicit publish   a local file. A `.html` file is checked against the floor below.
  read/list/comments no page is being written. Silent.

THE FLOOR IS SEVEN ITEMS AND IT IS NOT THE WHOLE RECIPE. It is the subset a reader LOSES
THE PAGE OVER: dark-mode handling that is absent or unguarded renders dark text on a dark
background for every viewer whose system is in dark mode, and the author — who published
from a light session — never sees it. Everything else the recipe asks for (the binder grid,
the section-number chips, the case-file strips, the pills) is a quality judgement the skill
makes and this script has no business adjudicating.

A DETECTOR SHIPS WITH ITS FIX (SKILL.md § Directives). The denial reason names each missing
item and the one action that resolves all of them, so the model never has to guess what
"apply the house style" means.

ZERO KNOWN WRONG FIRES BEFORE IT BLOCKS, and two of the user's own items are ADVISORY for
exactly that reason. MEASURED 2026-10-02 against the seven known-good artifact pages on this
machine, listed in `fixtures/scripts/artifact-style-*/README.md`:

  viewport meta       every artifact-published page in the corpus is a FRAGMENT with no
                      `<head>` of its own — the publisher supplies the document wrapper.
                      Requiring the meta unconditionally would have denied the very page the
                      user called wonderful. Required only where the page declares its own
                      `<head`, where the wrapper is the author's; 7 of 7 pass.
  a width media query `krull-makeover.html`, an accepted artifact, has none: it is a
                      side-by-side of projector slides and a narrow layout is meaningless
                      for it. ADVISORY, so it is always named and never blocks.

FAIL-OPEN, ALWAYS. Exit 0 on unparseable input, a path it cannot read, a payload shape it
does not recognise, or any exception — the same contract `wf-code-origin` states one file
over. A hook bug must never stand between the user and their own publish.
"""
import json
import os
import re
import sys

SKILL = "shared-artifact"

# EVERY PATTERN HERE RUNS AGAINST ONE SHORT FRAGMENT — a selector, a declaration block, or
# an at-rule prelude — never across the document, and that is a ReDoS fix rather than a
# tidy-up. MEASURED 2026-10-02 on the shapes this file used to carry: `@media[^{]*max-width`
# over a page with 20,000 `@media` tokens and a 400 KB brace-free tail took 96s, and
# `:root[^{]*\{` took 12s on the same input. The gap scan is O(n) per anchor and the anchors
# are O(n), so every one of them was quadratic. `css_blocks` walks the braces once and the
# patterns below then match inside fragments whose total length is the file's length.
ROOT_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root\b")
TOKEN_DECL_RX = re.compile(r"--[A-Za-z][\w-]*\s*:")
LIGHT_GUARD_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root:not\(\s*\[\s*data-theme\s*=\s*[\"']light[\"']\s*\]\s*\)")
# THE SECOND ACCEPTED FORM, AND IT IS WHY THE LIGHT CHECK HAS TWO. The recipe guards the dark
# media block with `:not([data-theme="light"])`; a page reaches the identical result by letting
# the media block set dark and restating the light tokens in a `:root[data-theme="light"]` rule
# after it. MEASURED 2026-10-02: `image-swap-proposal.html` does exactly that, and the
# single-form check denied it. A viewer forcing light gets light tokens either way, so denying
# the second form is a wrong fire on a page that is not broken.
LIGHT_OVERRIDE_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root\s*\[\s*data-theme\s*=\s*[\"']light[\"']\s*\]")
FORCED_DARK_SEL_RX = re.compile(r":root\s*\[\s*data-theme\s*=\s*[\"']dark[\"']\s*\]")
BODY_SEL_RX = re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s,>+~])body(?:\s|,|:|$)")
BACKGROUND_RX = re.compile(r"\bbackground(?:-color)?\s*:", re.I)
DARK_PRELUDE_RX = re.compile(r"prefers-color-scheme\s*:\s*dark", re.I)
WIDTH_PRELUDE_RX = re.compile(r"(?:max|min)-width", re.I)
STYLE_OPEN_RX = re.compile(r"<style[^>]*>", re.I)
STYLE_CLOSE = "</style"
TITLE_RX = re.compile(r"<title[^>]*>\s*\S[^<]*</title>", re.I)
HEAD_RX = re.compile(r"<head[\s>]", re.I)
VIEWPORT_RX = re.compile(r"name\s*=\s*[\"']viewport[\"']", re.I)

# The style instruction, in one place. It names the skill rather than restating the recipe:
# a second copy of the rules inside a hook is the copy that drifts, and the recipe lives in
# the skill where it is shipped, versioned and read.
APPLY = (
    "This project ships the house style for shareable online artifacts as the `%s` skill. "
    "Load it now and build the page to it before writing any markup." % SKILL
)
# The routing rule, which is the lesson of the two publishes of one brief. HTML can style its
# own headings; the Docs viewer cannot, because it owns them.
ROUTING = (
    "Routing: a polished read-only page people will READ goes to HTML — `quickstart` with "
    "intent \"other\", then one self-contained `.html` file. Claude Docs is the right "
    "destination only when co-editing or commenting in the document is the point, because "
    "the Docs viewer owns its heading styles and a brief published there cannot make its "
    "headings pop. Keep one source of truth: never a Docs copy and an HTML copy of the same "
    "content, because they drift."
)


def stylesheets(text):
    """Every `<style>` block's contents joined, or the whole document when there is none.

    SCOPED TO THE CSS ON PURPOSE. An artifact page carries its images inline, so a `:root`
    or a `body {` inside a base64 blob or a JavaScript string is not a rule, and reading
    the whole document also means reading every byte of those blobs.
    """
    out = []
    lowered = text.lower()
    for m in STYLE_OPEN_RX.finditer(text):
        end = lowered.find(STYLE_CLOSE, m.end())
        out.append(text[m.end():end if end != -1 else len(text)])
    return "\n".join(out) if out else text


def css_blocks(css):
    """(selectors, declarations, preludes) for the whole sheet, walked with `str.find`.

    NOT A REGEX, and the reason is measured. `([^{}]*)\\{([^{}]*)\\}` is quadratic over a
    brace-free region: on the 456 KB `image-swap-proposal.html` in the corpus, with its
    inline base64 images, `findall` had not returned after two minutes. This walk is one
    pass.

    An at-rule wrapper (`@media (…) { .wrap { … } }`) is stepped INTO, so the rule that
    carries the declarations is the one reported and the wrapper's own text is collected as
    a PRELUDE. The prelude is where `prefers-color-scheme: dark` and a width query live, and
    reading them here is what lets those two checks match a short string instead of scanning
    the document for a brace that may be half a megabyte away.
    """
    sels, decls, preludes = [], [], []
    sel_start = 0
    i = 0
    n = len(css)
    while i < n:
        o = css.find("{", i)
        if o < 0:
            break
        c = css.find("}", o + 1)
        nxt = css.find("{", o + 1)
        if nxt != -1 and (c == -1 or nxt < c):
            preludes.append(css[sel_start:o])
            sel_start = o + 1
            i = nxt
            continue
        if c < 0:
            break
        sels.append(css[sel_start:o])
        decls.append(css[o + 1:c])
        sel_start = i = c + 1
    return sels, decls, preludes


def any_rule(sels, decls, sel_rx, decl_rx=None):
    """True when some rule's selector matches, and its declarations match too when asked.

    The `decl_rx` half is what separates `:root { --bg: … }` from a bare `:root {}`: a
    selector with no custom properties under it restates no palette.
    """
    for sel, decl in zip(sels, decls):
        if sel_rx.search(sel) and (decl_rx is None or decl_rx.search(decl)):
            return True
    return False


def floor_failures(text):
    """(denials, advisories). Each entry already reads as an instruction."""
    bad = []
    sels, decls, preludes = css_blocks(stylesheets(text))
    if not any_rule(sels, decls, ROOT_SEL_RX, TOKEN_DECL_RX):
        bad.append("no `:root { --token: … }` custom properties — every colour in the page "
                   "comes from a token, so one block can restate them all for dark mode")
    if not any(DARK_PRELUDE_RX.search(_p) for _p in preludes):
        bad.append("no `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` block — the page renders in the "
                   "light palette for every viewer whose system is dark")
    if not (any_rule(sels, decls, LIGHT_GUARD_SEL_RX)
            or any_rule(sels, decls, LIGHT_OVERRIDE_SEL_RX, TOKEN_DECL_RX)):
        bad.append("a viewer who forces LIGHT still gets the dark tokens: guard the dark "
                   "block with `:root:not([data-theme=\"light\"])`, or restate the light "
                   "tokens in a `:root[data-theme=\"light\"]` rule after it")
    if not any_rule(sels, decls, FORCED_DARK_SEL_RX, TOKEN_DECL_RX):
        bad.append("no `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]` block — a viewer who forces dark outside "
                   "the system preference gets light tokens")
    if not any_rule(sels, decls, BODY_SEL_RX, BACKGROUND_RX):
        bad.append("`body` sets no background — the page inherits the host's, so dark ink "
                   "lands on a dark surface and the author publishing from a light session "
                   "never sees it")
    if not TITLE_RX.search(text):
        bad.append("no non-empty `<title>` — the title is the artifact's name in the share "
                   "list, and it should be 2 to 4 words")
    # CONDITIONAL, and the condition is measured. See the module header.
    if HEAD_RX.search(text) and not VIEWPORT_RX.search(text):
        bad.append("the page declares its own `<head` and carries no viewport meta, so it "
                   "cannot reflow on a phone — add "
                   "`<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">`")
    soft = []
    if not any(WIDTH_PRELUDE_RX.search(_p) for _p in preludes):
        soft.append("no width media query — check the page at about 390px and collapse the "
                    "contents rail to a static grid at the top there")
    return bad, soft


# THE REASON IS AN INSTRUCTION CHANNEL, AND ONLY FIXED TEXT GOES INTO IT. A PreToolUse
# `permissionDecisionReason` is read by the model as its next instruction, so anything echoed
# there is something the page could tell the model to do. The FLOOR ITEMS ARE ALL CONSTANTS
# and the PAGE'S CONTENT IS NEVER QUOTED — which matters because a published page legitimately
# carries text from somewhere else, and "the line that failed" would be a second-order
# injection straight into the instruction stream. The one value from outside this script that
# reaches the reason is the file's own name, bounded here.
NAME_SAFE_RX = re.compile(r"[^\w.\- ]", re.U)


def safe_name(path):
    return NAME_SAFE_RX.sub("", os.path.basename(path))[:80] or "the page"


def emit(payload):
    print(json.dumps(payload))


def context(text):
    emit({"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                                 "additionalContext": text}})


def deny(reason):
    emit({"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                                 "permissionDecision": "deny",
                                 "permissionDecisionReason": reason}})


def main():
    try:
        payload = json.loads(sys.stdin.read() or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        return 0
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return 0
    ti = payload.get("tool_input")
    if not isinstance(ti, dict):
        return 0
    action = (ti.get("action") or "").strip().lower()

    # No page is being written by any of these.
    if action in ("read", "list", "comments", "delete"):
        return 0

    if action == "quickstart":
        context(APPLY + " " + ROUTING)
        return 0

    # A connector-typed create. There is no local file to read and the viewer owns the
    # heading styles, so the only useful thing to say is where this belongs.
    if ti.get("type_url") and not ti.get("file_path"):
        context(ROUTING)
        return 0

    # An uploaded asset is a file being carried, not a page being styled.
    if ti.get("asset"):
        return 0

    path = ti.get("file_path")
    if not isinstance(path, str) or not path:
        return 0
    if not path.lower().endswith((".html", ".htm")):
        # Markdown, an image, a notebook: not this detector's subject. The style
        # instruction still goes out, because the recipe routes the choice of format.
        context(APPLY)
        return 0

    try:
        cwd = payload.get("cwd") or os.getcwd()
        full = path if os.path.isabs(path) else os.path.join(cwd, path)
        with open(full, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return 0
    if not text.strip():
        return 0

    bad, soft = floor_failures(text)
    if bad:
        deny("This page is about to be published without the house style for shareable "
             "artifacts, and %d item(s) of its floor are missing:\n%s\n\n%s Then rewrite "
             "%s so every item above is satisfied and publish again. The floor is the part "
             "a reader LOSES THE PAGE OVER, not a preference: unguarded dark mode renders "
             "dark text on a dark background for every viewer in dark mode, and you are "
             "publishing from a light session and will not see it."
             % (len(bad), "\n".join("  - " + b for b in bad), APPLY, safe_name(path)))
        return 0
    if soft:
        context("The page meets the shareable-artifact floor. Still open, and worth fixing "
                "before this is shared:\n%s" % "\n".join("  - " + s for s in soft))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — fail-open is the contract, stated in the header
        sys.exit(0)
